Lab Overview
In this advanced lab, you will implement a comprehensive group-based permission system that mirrors real-world enterprise environments. You'll create three distinct user groups with different access levels and test the security boundaries between them.

Task 1: Create User Groups
Subtask 1.1: Create Administrative Group
First, let's create the admins group that will have elevated privileges.

# Create the admins group
sudo groupadd admins

# Verify the group was created
getent group admins
Expected Output:

admins:x:1001:
Subtask 1.2: Create Developers Group
Create the developers group for users who need development access.

# Create the developers group
sudo groupadd developers

# Verify the group was created
getent group developers
Subtask 1.3: Create Viewers Group
Create the viewers group for users with read-only access.

# Create the viewers group
sudo groupadd viewers

# Verify all groups were created
getent group | grep -E "(admins|developers|viewers)"
Expected Output:

admins:x:1001:
developers:x:1002:
viewers:x:1003:
Subtask 1.4: Create Test Users
Create test users for each group to demonstrate permissions.

# Create admin user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash admin_user
sudo passwd admin_user

# Create developer user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash dev_user
sudo passwd dev_user

# Create viewer user
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash view_user
sudo passwd view_user
Note: Set simple passwords like Password123! for testing purposes.

Subtask 1.5: Add Users to Groups
Assign users to their respective groups.

# Add users to their primary groups
sudo usermod -a -G admins admin_user
sudo usermod -a -G developers dev_user
sudo usermod -a -G viewers view_user

# Verify group memberships
groups admin_user
groups dev_user
groups view_user
Task 2: Assign Appropriate Permissions for Each Group
Subtask 2.1: Create Directory Structure
Set up a directory structure that will demonstrate different permission levels.

# Create main project directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/company_project

# Create subdirectories for different access levels
sudo mkdir -p /opt/company_project/admin_only
sudo mkdir -p /opt/company_project/development
sudo mkdir -p /opt/company_project/public_docs

# Create sample files
sudo touch /opt/company_project/admin_only/system_config.txt
sudo touch /opt/company_project/development/source_code.py
sudo touch /opt/company_project/public_docs/readme.txt
Subtask 2.2: Configure Admin Group Permissions
Grant admins group full access to all directories and sudo privileges.

# Set ownership and permissions for admin directory
sudo chown root:admins /opt/company_project/admin_only
sudo chmod 770 /opt/company_project/admin_only
sudo chown root:admins /opt/company_project/admin_only/system_config.txt
sudo chmod 660 /opt/company_project/admin_only/system_config.txt

# Add admins group to sudoers
echo "%admins ALL=(ALL) ALL" | sudo tee -a /etc/sudoers.d/admins
Subtask 2.3: Configure Developers Group Permissions
Grant developers group access to development resources and limited system access.

# Set ownership and permissions for development directory
sudo chown root:developers /opt/company_project/development
sudo chmod 775 /opt/company_project/development
sudo chown root:developers /opt/company_project/development/source_code.py
sudo chmod 664 /opt/company_project/development/source_code.py

# Create sudoers file for developers with limited privileges
sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/developers << 'EOF'
%developers ALL=(ALL) /bin/systemctl status *, /bin/systemctl restart httpd, /bin/systemctl restart nginx, /usr/bin/tail /var/log/*
EOF
Subtask 2.4: Configure Viewers Group Permissions
Grant viewers group read-only access to public documentation.

# Set ownership and permissions for public docs
sudo chown root:viewers /opt/company_project/public_docs
sudo chmod 755 /opt/company_project/public_docs
sudo chown root:viewers /opt/company_project/public_docs/readme.txt
sudo chmod 644 /opt/company_project/public_docs/readme.txt

# Viewers get no sudo privileges (no sudoers entry needed)
Subtask 2.5: Set Up Advanced Permission Controls
Implement additional security measures using Access Control Lists (ACLs).

# Install ACL tools if not present
sudo yum install acl -y  # For RHEL/CentOS
# sudo apt-get install acl -y  # For Ubuntu/Debian

# Set ACLs for fine-grained control
sudo setfacl -m g:admins:rwx /opt/company_project/admin_only
sudo setfacl -m g:developers:r-x /opt/company_project/admin_only
sudo setfacl -m g:viewers:--- /opt/company_project/admin_only

sudo setfacl -m g:admins:rwx /opt/company_project/development
sudo setfacl -m g:developers:rwx /opt/company_project/development
sudo setfacl -m g:viewers:r-x /opt/company_project/development

# Verify ACLs
getfacl /opt/company_project/admin_only
getfacl /opt/company_project/development
Subtask 2.6: Create Group-Specific Scripts
Create scripts that demonstrate different permission levels.

# Create admin script
sudo tee /opt/company_project/admin_only/admin_script.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Admin Script - Full System Access"
echo "Current user: $(whoami)"
echo "Groups: $(groups)"
echo "System uptime: $(uptime)"
ps aux | head -10
EOF

sudo chmod 750 /opt/company_project/admin_only/admin_script.sh
sudo chown root:admins /opt/company_project/admin_only/admin_script.sh

# Create developer script
sudo tee /opt/company_project/development/dev_script.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Developer Script - Development Access"
echo "Current user: $(whoami)"
echo "Groups: $(groups)"
echo "Development environment check:"
python3 --version 2>/dev/null || echo "Python3 not installed"
git --version 2>/dev/null || echo "Git not installed"
EOF

sudo chmod 755 /opt/company_project/development/dev_script.sh
sudo chown root:developers /opt/company_project/development/dev_script.sh

# Create viewer script
sudo tee /opt/company_project/public_docs/view_script.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash
echo "Viewer Script - Read-Only Access"
echo "Current user: $(whoami)"
echo "Groups: $(groups)"
echo "Available documentation:"
ls -la /opt/company_project/public_docs/
EOF

sudo chmod 755 /opt/company_project/public_docs/view_script.sh
sudo chown root:viewers /opt/company_project/public_docs/view_script.sh
Task 3: Test User Access
Subtask 3.1: Test Admin User Access
Switch to the admin user and test their permissions.

# Switch to admin user
su - admin_user

# Test admin directory access
cd /opt/company_project/admin_only
ls -la
cat system_config.txt

# Test script execution
./admin_script.sh

# Test sudo privileges
sudo whoami

# Test access to other directories
ls -la /opt/company_project/development
ls -la /opt/company_project/public_docs

# Exit admin user session
exit
Subtask 3.2: Test Developer User Access
Switch to the developer user and verify their permissions.

# Switch to developer user
su - dev_user

# Test development directory access
cd /opt/company_project/development
ls -la
cat source_code.py

# Test script execution
./dev_script.sh

# Test limited sudo privileges
sudo systemctl status sshd

# Try to access admin directory (should fail)
cd /opt/company_project/admin_only
ls -la /opt/company_project/admin_only

# Test public docs access
ls -la /opt/company_project/public_docs

# Exit developer user session
exit
Subtask 3.3: Test Viewer User Access
Switch to the viewer user and test their read-only permissions.

# Switch to viewer user
su - view_user

# Test public docs access
cd /opt/company_project/public_docs
ls -la
cat readme.txt

# Test script execution
./view_script.sh

# Try to access restricted directories (should fail)
cd /opt/company_project/admin_only
cd /opt/company_project/development

# Try to use sudo (should fail)
sudo whoami

# Exit viewer user session
exit
Subtask 3.4: Create Permission Test Script
Create a comprehensive test script to verify all permissions.

sudo tee /opt/company_project/permission_test.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "=== Permission Testing Script ==="
echo "Testing access for all user groups"
echo

# Function to test directory access
test_directory_access() {
    local user=$1
    local directory=$2
    echo "Testing $user access to $directory:"
    
    sudo -u $user test -r "$directory" && echo "  ✓ Read access: YES" || echo "  ✗ Read access: NO"
    sudo -u $user test -w "$directory" && echo "  ✓ Write access: YES" || echo "  ✗ Write access: NO"
    sudo -u $user test -x "$directory" && echo "  ✓ Execute access: YES" || echo "  ✗ Execute access: NO"
    echo
}

# Test admin user
echo "=== ADMIN USER TESTS ==="
test_directory_access "admin_user" "/opt/company_project/admin_only"
test_directory_access "admin_user" "/opt/company_project/development"
test_directory_access "admin_user" "/opt/company_project/public_docs"

# Test developer user
echo "=== DEVELOPER USER TESTS ==="
test_directory_access "dev_user" "/opt/company_project/admin_only"
test_directory_access "dev_user" "/opt/company_project/development"
test_directory_access "dev_user" "/opt/company_project/public_docs"

# Test viewer user
echo "=== VIEWER USER TESTS ==="
test_directory_access "view_user" "/opt/company_project/admin_only"
test_directory_access "view_user" "/opt/company_project/development"
test_directory_access "view_user" "/opt/company_project/public_docs"

echo "=== Permission Testing Complete ==="
EOF

sudo chmod +x /opt/company_project/permission_test.sh
Subtask 3.5: Run Comprehensive Tests
Execute the permission test script to verify all configurations.

# Run the permission test script
